### What is the Minimum Meeting Rooms problem?

The problem gives a set of meetings, each with a start and end time, and asks for the minimum number of rooms needed so that no two overlapping meetings share a room. This is equivalent to finding the maximum number of meetings active at any single instant, because that peak is the moment when the most rooms are in use.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class MinMeetingRooms {
    public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] meetings) {
        if (meetings.length == 0) return 0;
        Arrays.sort(meetings, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));

        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(); // min-heap of end times
        heap.offer(meetings[0][1]);

        for (int i = 1; i < meetings.length; i++) {
            if (heap.peek() <= meetings[i][0]) {
                heap.poll(); // room freed
            }
            heap.offer(meetings[i][1]); // occupy a room
        }
        return heap.size();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] meetings = {{0,30},{5,10},{15,20}};
        System.out.println("Rooms needed: " + new MinMeetingRooms().minMeetingRooms(meetings));
    }
}
```

```text
Rooms needed: 2
```

### Why does the heap give the correct answer?

The min-heap holds the end time of each currently occupied room. Before seating a new meeting, we check the root: if the soonest-ending room finishes at or before the new meeting's start, that room is free and we poll it. Then we offer the new meeting's end time. Because we only free rooms that genuinely ended, the heap size at any moment equals the number of concurrent meetings. The maximum heap size over the entire run is the answer.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class HeapTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] meetings = {{0,30},{5,10},{15,20}};
        Arrays.sort(meetings, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));
        PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();

        for (int[] m : meetings) {
            if (!heap.isEmpty() && heap.peek() <= m[0]) {
                System.out.println("Free room ending at " + heap.poll());
            }
            heap.offer(m[1]);
            System.out.println("Seat [" + m[0] + "," + m[1] + "] heap size=" + heap.size());
        }
        System.out.println("Max rooms: " + heap.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Seat [0,30] heap size=1
Seat [5,10] heap size=2
Free room ending at 10
Seat [15,20] heap size=2
Max rooms: 2
```

### What is the two-pointer sweep alternative?

Separate all start times and all end times into two sorted arrays. Walk a start pointer and an end pointer. When the next event is a start, increment a running room count and update the maximum. When the next event is an end, decrement the room count. The peak running count is the answer. This avoids the heap.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class TwoPointerMeetingRooms {
    public int minMeetingRooms(int[][] meetings) {
        int n = meetings.length;
        int[] starts = new int[n];
        int[] ends = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            starts[i] = meetings[i][0];
            ends[i] = meetings[i][1];
        }
        Arrays.sort(starts);
        Arrays.sort(ends);

        int rooms = 0, maxRooms = 0;
        int s = 0, e = 0;
        while (s < n) {
            if (starts[s] < ends[e]) {
                rooms++;
                maxRooms = Math.max(maxRooms, rooms);
                s++;
            } else {
                rooms--;
                e++;
            }
        }
        return maxRooms;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] meetings = {{0,30},{5,10},{15,20}};
        System.out.println("Rooms (two-pointer): "
            + new TwoPointerMeetingRooms().minMeetingRooms(meetings));
    }
}
```

```text
Rooms (two-pointer): 2
```

### How does the tie-handling work?

When a meeting ends exactly as another starts, whether they can share a room depends on the domain. Using `<=` in `heap.peek() <= start` means a room freed at time 10 can host a meeting starting at 10 (back-to-back reuse). Using `<` means the room is still occupied at 10 and a new room is needed.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class TieHandling {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Meeting A: [0, 10], Meeting B: [10, 20]
        // <= : room freed at 10, B starts at 10 => same room (1 room)
        // <  : room still busy at 10 => need 2 rooms

        System.out.println("<= (back-to-back reuse): 1 room for [0,10] and [10,20]");
        System.out.println("<  (no reuse at exact end): 2 rooms");
    }
}
```

### How is this different from Merge Intervals?

| Feature | Merge Intervals | Meeting Rooms |
|---|---|---|
| Goal | merge overlapping intervals | count peak overlap |
| Output | merged interval list | integer (room count) |
| Technique | sort + sweep + extend | sort + heap or two-pointer |
| Key insight | max-end rule | min rooms = max overlap |

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MergeVsMeetingRooms {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Merge: combine overlapping, return merged list.");
        System.out.println("Meeting Rooms: count peak overlap, return integer.");
    }
}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include no meetings (0 rooms), one meeting (1 room), all meetings at the same time (n rooms), and no overlapping meetings (1 room).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MeetingRoomEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MinMeetingRooms solver = new MinMeetingRooms();

        System.out.println("No meetings: " + solver.minMeetingRooms(new int[][]{}));
        System.out.println("One meeting: " + solver.minMeetingRooms(new int[][]{{0,10}}));
        System.out.println("All overlap: " + solver.minMeetingRooms(
            new int[][]{{0,10},{0,10},{0,10}}));
        System.out.println("Sequential: " + solver.minMeetingRooms(
            new int[][]{{0,5},{5,10},{10,15}}));
    }
}
```

```text
No meetings: 0
One meeting: 1
All overlap: 3
Sequential: 1
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not sorting the meetings by start time before processing. Another mistake is freeing multiple rooms in one step instead of just the one whose end time is earliest.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: not sorted, processes meetings in arbitrary order
for (int[] m : meetings) {
    heap.offer(m[1]);
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: sort first, then free-and-occupy
Arrays.sort(meetings, Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[0]));
for (int[] m : meetings) {
    if (!heap.isEmpty() && heap.peek() <= m[0]) heap.poll();
    heap.offer(m[1]);
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that "minimum rooms equals maximum overlap" is the key conceptual leap. They also know this exact model applies to CPU thread scheduling, connection pool sizing, airport gate assignment, and any resource allocation problem about peak simultaneous demand. Both the heap and two-pointer approaches run in O(n log n), and the choice depends on whether subsequent operations need the individual room assignments (heap) or just the count (two-pointer).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorMeetingRoomNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Senior insights:");
        System.out.println("  min rooms = max overlap (key insight).");
        System.out.println("  Applies: CPU scheduling, connection pools, gate assignment.");
        System.out.println("  Heap: O(n log n), gives room assignments.");
        System.out.println("  Two-pointer: O(n log n), gives count only.");
    }
}
```
